The reinforced bunker had been hastily modified overnight. Steel bands now crisscrossed the walls and ceiling, supplementing the original containment systems. Chip had demanded\'97and received\'97additional shielding, though he kept insisting it was inadequate for what they were proposing.\
"You're being ridiculous," The Johnny protested as the assembled group filed into the observation room. "We've tested everything now. We know what to expect."\
"No," Chip countered firmly, "we know what happened once. Under different conditions. The multi-disc resonance experiment has never been attempted."\
On the bunker floor, three Gyges Discs sat in triangular formation, each mounted on its own spinning apparatus. The configuration had been Chilo's inspired suggestion that morning, and somehow The Johnny had become its biggest advocate.\
"Three discs should amplify the temporal effects exponentially," The Johnny explained to Eloise, who had insisted on personally observing this test. "If our calculations are correct\'97"\
"Your calculations are theoretical extrapolations," Chip interrupted, pointing at his tablet. "Based on single-disc data. Three discs in resonance could create a feedback loop we can't control."\
"Or," Chilo suggested quietly, "they could create a stable portal. A controlled link between universes."\
Solo frowned. "Between universes traveling at different temporal speeds? That's like trying to merge three rivers flowing in different directions."\
"But the potential\'97" The Johnny began.\
"Is catastrophic," Chip finished. "I'm formally on record as opposing this experiment. The bunker's structural integrity calculations don't account for triple-resonance harmonics."\
Kleoboulos moved to stand beside Chip. "For once, I agree with the refrigeration expert. This goes beyond acceptable risk parameters."\
Eloise studied the bunker through the new observation window, calculating. "Define 'catastrophic,' Mr. Stockwell."\
Chip's knuckles whitened around his tablet. "Best case? Localized spacetime distortion that destroys the facility. Worst case? We create a cascade effect that ripples across multiple universes. Temporal paradoxes. Reality destabilization. Possibly worse."\
"And best case for success?"\
"Controlled interdimensional communication. Possibly energy transfer between universes. Technology that could revolutionize\'97" The Johnny stopped when he saw Chip's expression.\
"Don't lecture him on potential, John," Eloise said. "Lecture me. Convince me this is worth destroying my facility."\
The Johnny puffed out his chest. "Because we'll learn things humanity was never meant to know. Because profit follows progress. Because\'97"\
A ping from Sarah's tablet interrupted him. She'd been monitoring remotely from her quarters. "I'm seeing the patterns again," her voice crackled through the speaker. "The same ones from my nightmares. More intense than before. This feels wrong."\
"Miss Jacobson," Chilo addressed the speaker, "sometimes growth requires discomfort. Evolution demands risk."\
"This isn't evolution," Sarah replied sharply. "It's playing with matches in a dynamite factory."\
Eloise checked her watch. "Decision time. Mr. Stockwell, if we implemented every safeguard you've recommended, how long would this test take?"\
"Six months minimum, with extensive computer modeling."\
"And without?"\
"Forty minutes," The Johnny answered eagerly.\
Eloise nodded once. "We're go for the test in thirty minutes. Mr. Stockwell, you'll monitor structural integrity. Dr. Quark, temporal variance. John, you're at primary controls. If anything deviates from projected parameters by more than twenty percent, immediate shutdown."\
"Finally!" The Johnny practically skipped to his station.\
As technicians made final preparations, Kleoboulos caught Eloise's arm. "You're making a mistake."\
"Perhaps," she admitted quietly. "But the alternative is watching someone else make this mistake first. With our technology."\
"And if John's ambition outweighs his caution?"\
She smiled grimly. "Then that's why I'm here."\
In the bunker, the three discs began to spin simultaneously. The rainbow refractions immediately danced across the walls, more vibrant than with a single disc. As the speed increased, the patterns began to merge, creating interlocking geometric forms that seemed to extend beyond the physical boundaries of the chamber.\
"5,000 RPM," The Johnny reported, excitement barely contained. "Temporal variance registering at... two percent? That's less than with single disc operation!"\
"Structural stress within acceptable parameters," Chip confirmed reluctantly, eyes glued to his screens.\
Sarah's voice crackled again: "The patterns... they're three-dimensional now. Moving through space. I can see them with my eyes closed."\
At 8,000 RPM, reality began to perceptibly warp. The three discs created a central vortex where space itself seemed to fold inward. Through the observation window, they watched coffee cups levitate, spin, and age decades before crumbling to dust.\
"Temporal distortion increasing exponentially," Chip warned. "We're approaching the danger threshold."\
"But look!" The Johnny pointed at the holographic displays. "The signal clarity is unprecedented. We're receiving structured data from all three universes simultaneously!"\
Eloise leaned forward. In the vortex's center, shapes were forming\'97geometric patterns that couldn't exist in three-dimensional space. Mathematical impossibilities made real.\
"10,000 RPM approaching," Dr. Quark announced. "Temporal variance at forty-seven percent!"\
"Shutdown protocols engaged\'97" Chip began, but The Johnny overrode him.\
"Not yet! We're so close to breakthrough!"\
The vortex suddenly pulsed, and everyone felt the world shift. For an instant, they existed in multiple places simultaneously\'97seeing the bunker as it had been, as it was, and as it might be. Through the warped space, they glimpsed fragments of other realities: the same facility in different configurations, the Gyges Discs in alien settings, beings that weren't human conducting similar experiments.\
"The universes are synchronizing," Chilo breathed, awestruck.\
"They're trying to occupy the same space!" Chip shouted. "Shutdown! Now!"\
As The Johnny reluctantly initiated emergency protocols, a final image crystallized in the vortex: a figure standing where their bunker floor should be, watching them with eyes that held infinite understanding\'97and infinite warning.\
The discs wound down, the vortex collapsed, and reality reasserted itself. But everyone knew something fundamental had changed. They'd glimpsed the space between universes, and perhaps something in that space had glimpsed them back.\
In the aftermath, as smoke alarms wailed and emergency systems activated, one thing was clear: they had proven controlled interdimensional contact was possible. The question remained whether any control they possessed was sufficient for what they had unleashed.\
"We'll run it again tomorrow," The Johnny announced, his eyes bright with possibility.\
Chip didn't argue. They'd crossed a threshold from which retreat was likely impossible.}